=base:main-content.cmark

# Getting started with microdns

microdns serves fixed records from a file and everything else from memory.
That is, you configure it after it has started, by calling an HTTP(S) API,
and it loses those records if it restarts.

## Run the example

microdns is a Haskell cabal project. From a checkout of
[the repository](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/microdns):

```sh
cabal update
echo unsafe > secret-web-hmac-file.txt
cabal run -- microdns plain \
  --dnsPort 5354 --dnsApex example.com. \
  --webPort 3333 --webHmacSecretFile secret-web-hmac-file.txt \
  --zoneFile fixtures/example.mzone
```

Then, from another terminal:

```sh
dig +short @127.0.0.1 -p 5354 example.com
```

answers `1.2.3.4`, the `A` record in
[`fixtures/example.mzone`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/microdns/blob/main/fixtures/example.mzone).

The options are:

- `--dnsPort`: the UDP port answering DNS queries.
- `--dnsApex`: the delegated DNS apex.
- `--webPort`: the TCP port of the configuration API.
- `--webHmacSecretFile`: the file holding the shared secret that
  authenticates [dynamic registrations](/dynamic-registration.html).
- `--zoneFile`: the [MicroZone file](/microzone-files.html) with the fixed
  records.

The `tls` command takes the same options plus `--certFile` and `--keyFile`,
and serves the configuration API over HTTPS.

## Running it for real

Unless you have really good reasons not to, run microdns on UDP port 53. You
also need to listen on a TCP port of your choice for configuration requests.
Use TLS to protect the API calls: the current code is sensitive to replay
attacks.

The HMAC mechanism is not very strong, so you should probably not expose the
configuration API on the Internet if your domain is a sensitive target. For
tech-savvy individuals with low-profile blogs, it is probably enough.

Assuming some Linux, the best is to run chrooted, with only enough
privileges to do networking and read the certificate and HMAC files.

Say `microdns` is installed in `/usr/local/bin`: you can grant the binary
the right to listen on port 53 even for non-root users with Linux
capabilities (`man 7 capabilities`).

```console
setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' /usr/local/bin/microdns
```

Also consider rate-limiting incoming and outgoing UDP, as DNS over UDP is
prone to abuse. An example rate limit, using netfilter:

```console
nft add rule inet filter input udp dport 53 limit rate over 10/second drop
```

## Where to go next

- [Dynamic registration](/dynamic-registration.html): add records over
  HTTP(S).
- [MicroZone files](/microzone-files.html): the format of the fixed records.

